Blars Uucp for Osk			March 9, 1992This is distributed as-is, it works for me (on my Qt20x running osk2.3) (the other ports of uucp for osk did not) but may not for you.  Ihave transfered files to and from all unix systems I've tried, and oneMSDOS one.  If I ever get around to putting much more work into UUCPfor Os9, I'll probably start from scratch porting Ion Taylors version. This is a uucp clone for os9/68k.  Since the uucico trafer protocoldoes not have any way to indicate binary or text files, all files arealways transfed in binary mode.  The uucp and uux programs by defaultconvert from osk style line endings (CR) to unix style (LF) onoutgoing files unless a binary option is specified.  Incoming fileswill arive with unix style line endings.  (The trlf program is usefulto convert.)  (One of many bugs I removed from this implementation wasthe inability to transfer binary files.)Blarslib is required.  Trlf and Cron are recomended.Mail is not included with this distribution because I have not yet gota version I consider fully functional.  A fully functional mail packagewill probably be big enough to distribute separatly anyway.I recomend the books "Managing uucp and usenet" and "Using uucp andusenet" for a good overview of what uucp is about and how to configureit.  If your local bookstore doesn't have them you can order themdirectly from O'reilly associates.I've dropped all the documentation that was no longer correct for thisversion, I felt it would be more confusing than useful.maker is a shell command to run make.  Needed because of bugs in make.You can ask questions of me at blarson@usc.edu.  No guarenteed answer.Setup:/h0/Ulib/uucp needs two files:Systems:lgnp1 Never /t2 HAYES 19200 g OK ATS50=255S110=0S111=30DT12153489727 CONNECT "" ogin: nuucpuunet Never /t2 HAYES 19200 g OK ATS50=255S110=0S111=30DT19004687727 CONNECT "" ogin: uucptest Never /t2 HAYES 19200 g OK ATCommands:# legal commands for uuxqt# rmailrnews/h0/spool/uucp contains spool directories for each system and .Errorand .Log subdirectories.Typical entries in crontab:23,53 * * * * uucico -r1 ^127 >>>/nil21 2,6,10,14,18,22 * * * uupoll abode02 23 * * * uupoll mcws